today I am convening the EU finance  ministers to agree on a bold response to  the coffee nineteen out break it is  arguably the most sizeable and ambitious  package ever prepared by the Eurogroup  it builds on unprecedented efforts  already taken by governments and  monetary authorities now we need a  European plan to help protect the most  affected and make sure the single market  will come out unarmed from this crisis  first a safety net for workers the  Commission proposed a 100 billion  program to finance employment protection  schemes second a safety net for  businesses the European Investment Bank  proposed the 200 billion guarantee to  support ailing firms especially SMEs  third a safety net for countries our  right to fund DSM has proposed  precautionary credit lines of up to 240  billion to ensure all countries have  adequate resources to respond this  emergency plan will shield our economic  and social tissue as we dive into a  recession but it cannot be separated  from the recovery which will shape the  Union for years to come this is not just  about surviving the virus it is about  getting our economies back on their feet  so today I will also call on ministers  to make a clear commitment for a  coordinated and sizeable recovery plan  we know we all know this is not time for  business as usual politics we must show  our citizens that Europe protects them  for them we must stand tall
 

